      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.28896_p1_b8",
      "text": "Using Gemma-2-9B with LoRA ranks r \u2208{4, 8, 16, 32}, we train adapter-specific SAEs across\nmultiple transformer layers and compare their learned feature spaces with pretrained SAE\ndictionaries. We evaluate representational alignment using cosine similarity between decoder\ndirections, principal-angle analysis of feature subspaces, and Centered Kernel Alignment\n(CKA) between activation representations. Across layers and ranks, we consistently observe\n",
      "bbox": [
        98.11,
        409.53,
        496.32,
        467.43
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        392,
        1638,
        1985,
        1869
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.86,
      "font_name": "CMR10",
      "is_math": true,
      "char_count": 451,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.28896_p1_b9",
      "text": "comparatively weak geometric alignment between LoRA-induced feature dictionaries and\npretrained SAE features. Adapter-specific SAEs also reconstruct delta activations more\neffectively than pretrained SAEs, suggesting that LoRA updates occupy partially distinct\nrepresentational structure within the residual stream. Additionally, feature density increases\nwith rank and depth, while geometric divergence remains relatively stable across ranks. These\nfindings provide empirical evidence that LoRA fine-tuning can induce feature structures\nthat are not fully captured by pretrained interpretability dictionaries, with implications for\nmechanistic interpretability, adaptation analysis, and safety auditing of fine-tuned language\nmodels.\n",
      "bbox": [
        98.91,
